uTerm: draw the echo's frame right away too, not just the key press's
(libghostty-spm patch 0026)

Drop this file into libghostty-spm's Patches/ghostty/ (after 0025);
build.sh applies it with `git apply` on every target. It is cut against
libghostty-spm 2.2.2026112401 with 0001-0025 applied, Ghostty 56de3f0f.

What it does (UTERM-PATCHES.md, "按键和它的回显都当场画"):
  - Patch 0025 draws the first frame after the display link was idle
    without waiting for vsync. A key press takes that frame for itself:
    `Surface.keyCallback` queues a render and so does the write to the
    pty, the renderer thread wakes twice (+0.2 and +0.4 ms), and every
    `updateFrame` rebuilds cells and draws, changed or not (`rebuildCells`
    always sets `cells_rebuilt`). Those frames are identical to the one on
    screen, the first starts the display link, and the echo, parsed at
    ~+0.8 ms, finds the link running and waits for its first tick, which
    after a start is one to two refresh intervals away.
  - renderer/Thread.zig renderCallback: updates come in bursts, and the
    first four of a burst are drawn now (`immediate_draws`): the two from
    the key press and the echo in one or two pieces, or a pointer position
    of a selection drag. A burst starts when the display link was idle, or
    when the renderer thread had nothing to do for 4 ms (`last_update_end`;
    the next key of fast typing arrives while the link started by the last
    one is still running). Continuous output keeps the link running and
    the thread busy, so it gets one burst and then stays at one frame per
    refresh.
  - The lines patch 0025 added are left exactly as they are (the code is
    added before and after them): apply-patches.sh recognises an applied
    patch by reversing it, so a later patch must not edit its hunks.

Only macOS has a display link (`hasVsync()` is always false on iOS, Mac
Catalyst and visionOS, where every update already draws at once), so this
changes nothing there. Rendering only: snapshot format and `src/terminal`
untouched.

Measurements: UTERM-PATCHES.md.

diff --git a/src/renderer/Thread.zig b/src/renderer/Thread.zig
index 1a6ea91..e2f01a3 100644
--- a/src/renderer/Thread.zig
+++ b/src/renderer/Thread.zig
@@ -82,6 +82,12 @@ mailbox: *Mailbox,
 /// Configuration we need derived from the main config.
 config: DerivedConfig,
 
+/// uTerm (patch 0026): frames drawn right away, without waiting for the
+/// display link, in the current burst of updates, and when the last update
+/// finished. See `renderCallback`.
+immediate_draws: u8 = 0,
+last_update_end: ?std.Io.Timestamp = null,
+
 flags: packed struct {
     /// This is true when a blinking cursor should be visible and false
     /// when it should not be visible. This is toggled on a timer by the
@@ -557,6 +563,14 @@ fn renderCallback(
         return .disarm;
     };
 
+    // uTerm (patch 0026): how long this thread sat idle before this update;
+    // used at the end of this function.
+    const update_start: std.Io.Timestamp = .now(global.io(), .awake);
+    const quiet_ns: i96 = if (t.last_update_end) |end|
+        end.durationTo(update_start).nanoseconds
+    else
+        std.math.maxInt(i96);
+
     // If the display is now unrealized, release GPU resources now
     // we're on the render thread, and do not try to update and draw
     // this frame.
@@ -596,6 +610,34 @@ fn renderCallback(
     // Schedule the next animation wake, if the renderer needs one.
     t.armAnimationTimer();
 
+    // uTerm (patch 0026): the echo's frame is drawn now too, not just the
+    // first frame after idle (patch 0025 above). A key press wakes this
+    // thread by itself, twice (`Surface.keyCallback` queues a render, and
+    // so does the write to the pty), and every update rebuilds cells and
+    // draws, changed or not. Those frames took the one immediate draw and
+    // started the display link ~0.5 ms before the echo was parsed, so the
+    // echo found the link running and waited for its first tick, which
+    // after a start is one to two refresh intervals away.
+    //
+    // So updates come in bursts, and the first `max_immediate_draws` of a
+    // burst are drawn now: the two from the key press and the echo in one
+    // or two pieces, or a pointer position of a selection drag.
+    // `drawFrame(link_was_idle)` above did nothing when the link was
+    // already running, so those are drawn here. A burst starts when the
+    // link was idle, or when this thread had nothing to do for
+    // `burst_gap_ns` (the next key of fast typing arrives while the link
+    // from the last one is still running). Continuous output keeps the
+    // link running and this thread busy, so it gets one burst and then
+    // stays at one frame per refresh.
+    const max_immediate_draws = 4;
+    const burst_gap_ns = 4 * std.time.ns_per_ms;
+    if (link_was_idle or quiet_ns >= burst_gap_ns) t.immediate_draws = 0;
+    if (t.immediate_draws < max_immediate_draws) {
+        t.immediate_draws += 1;
+        if (!link_was_idle) t.drawFrame(true);
+    }
+    t.last_update_end = std.Io.Timestamp.now(global.io(), .awake);
+
     return .disarm;
 }
 
